WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.467 --> 00:00:09.666
Hello, my name is Karolin Luger.

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:13.366
I'm a structural biologist at the University of Colorado Boulder

00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:17.699
and an investigator for the Howard Hughes Medical Institute.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.066
And today, I want to tell you a coup...

00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:22.132
about a couple of key experiments

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:26.032
that enabled a breakthrough in our decade-long efforts

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:30.232
to determine a three-dimensional structure of the nucleosome.

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:32.299
But before I do this, I want to highlight

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.899
the importance of this problem at that time.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.366
The human genome is over two meters in length

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:40.299
and has to fit into a nucleus

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.299
that's about five microns in size.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.299
A good analogy for this would be if you had

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.832
to fit a human hair that is ten miles long

00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:50.999
into a golf ball.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.266
And you... now imagine that you have to take that hair

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:58.866
and split it into two during a process called DNA replication,

00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:01.766
or imagine you have to find certain bits of information

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:03.999
on that piece of hair,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.466
all the while leaving the hair in the golf ball

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:10.999
and avoiding tangles or knots.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.666
A good analogy for using DNA

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:16.666
as an informational molecule is a cassette tape.

00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:19.532
That was used in prehistoric times by humans

00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:22.332
to record music and play music.

00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:25.066
Just like the cassette tape,

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:28.632
human genome packaging poses many challenges.

00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:30.966
We have to package the information

00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:32.532
in a very confined space.

00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:35.866
We have to protect it from physical damages and tangles.

00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:38.632
We have to regulate access to the stored information --

00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:41.432
so, you need to find your favorite ABBA song on your tape.

00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:44.699
You need to be able to tag that somehow and find it rapidly.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.099
And we also have to accurately and rapidly

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.366
replicate the information that is encoded on the tape --

00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:54.799
let's say you want to share that tape with your friends.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.232
Early views of human genome packaging

00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:00.099
came from taking chromosomes out of cells

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.799
and spreading them very, very thinly

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.666
on electron microsco... microscopy grids.

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:11.599
This revealed that the fundamental organizing units of chromatin

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.332
are spherical, about 10-nanometer-sized particles.

00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:21.732
These can then be separated out

00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:23.866
from the chromosome

00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:25.632
by using an enzyme called micrococcal nuclease.

00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:27.799
This enzyme cuts unprotected DNA,

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.232
but it will not cut the particles themselves.

00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:32.732
And then further analyses of these particles

00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:35.199
revealed that they're composed of DNA,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.899
roughly 150 base pairs of DNA,

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.499
and four to five small, very basic histone proteins.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:46.666
Efforts were undertaken to determine the structure of the nucleosome

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.132
by X-ray crystallography,

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:51.299
which was the method of choice in the 20th century.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.932
This method entails taking your molecule

00:02:53.933 --> 00:02:56.099
that you're interested in

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.566
and convincing it to form ordered crystals.

00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:02.332
You do this by subtly changing the solvent conditions

00:03:02.333 --> 00:03:04.766
around the molecule to...

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:08.032
molecules to convince them to aggregate with each other

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:09.799
in an ordered manner

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.732
to form these beautiful structures here,

00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:13.499
as shown in this green droplet.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.299
These crystals are about 100-500 micrometers in size,

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.099
so they're very, very small.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.632
So, you use a microscope to fish them out

00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:24.732
with a tiny little loop -- it's almost like a spoon --

00:03:24.733 --> 00:03:27.432
to mount them in this crystal loop.

00:03:27.433 --> 00:03:30.699
This loop is then immersed in liquid nitrogen

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.732
to rapidly vitrify the solvent around it

00:03:33.733 --> 00:03:37.432
and is then mounted on a goniometer head to be...

00:03:37.433 --> 00:03:41.899
to be illuminated by X-rays.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.732
The electrons in our molecule

00:03:43.733 --> 00:03:45.399
will diffract the X-rays,

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.299
and those diffracted X-rays

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.866
are collected on either a film

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:52.999
or, in later times, by electronic media.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.499
And it is this diffraction pattern that then,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.766
through a series of quite complicated

00:03:57.767 --> 00:04:01.232
mathematical operations that I will not go into,

00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:04.832
will then give rise to what we call

00:04:04.833 --> 00:04:06.232
the electron density map.

00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:08.366
And this electron density map

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:10.999
is then used to build your macromolecule,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.499
which then allows you to identify

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.232
the location of every atom

00:04:16.233 --> 00:04:18.699
with respect to every other atom.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.266
Now, you hear crystallographers talking a lot about

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:23.866
high resolution.

00:04:23.867 --> 00:04:25.899
High resolution is really needed

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.332
to understand function.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.932
What does that mean?

00:04:28.933 --> 00:04:31.999
Imagine you're an alien and you're trying to figure out

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.132
the function of a human hand.

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:37.232
Imagine now all you... all the...

00:04:37.233 --> 00:04:39.132
the only information that you have is a... is a mitten.

00:04:39.133 --> 00:04:40.832
You find a mitten on the road,

00:04:40.833 --> 00:04:43.332
and from that you conclude that humans

00:04:43.333 --> 00:04:45.332
have a large paddle-like protrusion

00:04:45.333 --> 00:04:47.066
and a smaller protrusion,

00:04:47.067 --> 00:04:49.066
and this constitutes the human hand.

00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:50.932
Now, imagine that you're finding a ski mitten.

00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:53.399
You're getting quite a lot of...

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.166
more resolution.

00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:56.866
You now know that humans have five fingers.

00:04:56.867 --> 00:04:58.999
But it's still quite limited

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.932
in the details that you can observe.

00:05:02.933 --> 00:05:05.432
The highest resolution structure you would obtain

00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:07.199
from a latex glove,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.899
and there you can figure out details in the finger joints,

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:14.799
and you could begin to understand how the human hand functions.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.232
This also illustrates a major problem

00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:19.199
that we have in crystallography,

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.966
because the images that we obtain

00:05:21.967 --> 00:05:23.832
-- the structures that we obtain --

00:05:23.833 --> 00:05:25.266
are static images.

00:05:25.267 --> 00:05:26.766
And from static images,

00:05:26.767 --> 00:05:28.732
it's sometimes hard to understand functions.

00:05:28.733 --> 00:05:30.566
And that's why crystallographers

00:05:30.567 --> 00:05:32.399
and other structural biologists

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.099
always use orthogonal approaches

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:38.932
to obtain different states of the molecule in question

00:05:38.933 --> 00:05:41.999
so that we can interpolate between those different states

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.966
to see how they get from here to there.

00:05:43.967 --> 00:05:45.866
And we also use additional methods

00:05:45.867 --> 00:05:49.799
to observe these machines and molecules

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:54.466
in the act of doing their job.

00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:55.999
Now, back to resolution.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.399
What determines the resolution in a crystal?

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.166
It is determined by order.

00:06:02.167 --> 00:06:04.199
A single crystal is contained...

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:08.366
is composed of billions and billions of macromolecules.

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:10.699
In the ideal case, all macromolecules

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.132
would look the same,

00:06:12.133 --> 00:06:14.066
as seen in the example to the left side.

00:06:14.067 --> 00:06:16.366
We have all of our macromolecules

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:17.932
very happy and smiling,

00:06:17.933 --> 00:06:20.399
and so the average structure that we get

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.299
from that crystal lattice

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.632
would be a smiley.

00:06:24.633 --> 00:06:27.566
And this would be a very high resolution structure.

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:29.732
In contrast, if some of our macromolecules

00:06:29.733 --> 00:06:31.999
are not happy -- they're frownies --

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.966
they are incorporated into the crystals,

00:06:33.967 --> 00:06:35.766
and what we get from these crystals

00:06:35.767 --> 00:06:39.132
is a composite of smileys and frownies.

00:06:39.133 --> 00:06:41.866
And so, we really have a hard time

00:06:41.867 --> 00:06:43.999
to determine what their facial expression is.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.299
And this is what would constitute a low resolution structure.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.299
Now, back to nucleosomes.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:55.366
The way nucleosomes were isolated in previous days

00:06:55.367 --> 00:07:01.099
was by isolating chromatin from natural sources

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.332
-- from chicken blood or calf thymus --

00:07:04.333 --> 00:07:06.599
and by cutting out nucleosomes

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.166
with micrococcal nuclease

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:10.899
and using those for crystallization.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:12.566
Now, this posed a problem,

00:07:12.567 --> 00:07:15.099
because by definition the DNA sequence of every single nucleosome

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.699
would be different,

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.499
because we sampled the entire genome.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.599
The DNA length would also be subtly different

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.066
because the enzyme isn't that accurate

00:07:24.067 --> 00:07:26.032
in chewing back all the way.

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:27.899
And the histone components, the protein components,

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.166
are also highly variable

00:07:30.167 --> 00:07:32.899
because they exist in many flavors.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.232
These many flavors have very, very important biological implications,

00:07:36.233 --> 00:07:39.266
but as far as crystallography goes,

00:07:39.267 --> 00:07:42.499
they really represent a big problem

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:46.466
because they preclude the formation of highly diffracting crystals

00:07:46.467 --> 00:07:50.266
because the material is just simply too heterogeneous.

00:07:50.267 --> 00:07:52.399
And so, this could be...

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:54.299
this should come as no surprise,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.766
that the first efforts to determine the 7-Ang...

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:58.532
to determine the structure of the nucleosome

00:07:58.533 --> 00:08:00.066
were limited to 7 Angstroms.

00:08:00.067 --> 00:08:02.966
So, this would be the equivalent of a mitten.

00:08:02.967 --> 00:08:06.532
And we really couldn't see a lot of detail from these structures,

00:08:06.533 --> 00:08:09.299
but we could discern that the DNA

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.399
was wrapped around the outside of the protein.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.032
The protein constituted a dense core.

00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:19.366
And we could discern the super helical path of the DNA

00:08:19.367 --> 00:08:21.699
around the outside of the cylinder.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.599
Details were not distinguishable.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.032
We... this structure did not allow us

00:08:27.033 --> 00:08:29.599
to determine how the histones hold to the DNA

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:33.999
and how they afford this quite profound distortion.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.166
I should also point out that

00:08:37.167 --> 00:08:38.832
these low resolution structure,

00:08:38.833 --> 00:08:40.366
more than anything else,

00:08:40.367 --> 00:08:42.299
rely on orthogonal methods to verify the conclusions

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.899
that we draw from them.

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.266
And in this particular case,

00:08:45.267 --> 00:08:47.332
very beautiful cross-linking data

00:08:47.333 --> 00:08:49.332
from the Mirzabekov lab

00:08:49.333 --> 00:08:55.299
really helped us to verify that this structure was true.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.099
Now, how do we get to higher resolution?

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.999
How do we get from these diffraction pattern

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.332
that have very...

00:09:03.333 --> 00:09:05.466
only very few diffraction spots

00:09:05.467 --> 00:09:08.866
to these beautiful Starry Night-like-looking diffraction patterns,

00:09:08.867 --> 00:09:11.199
where we have lots of information,

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.199
and as we...

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.399
as we say, they diffract to very high resolution?

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:19.199
There’s three fundamental improvements that had to be made.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.299
First and most importantly, sample homogeneity.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:24.332
So, we had to convince our nucleosomes

00:09:24.333 --> 00:09:25.832
all to be happy and smiley,

00:09:25.833 --> 00:09:28.232
and this proved to be the most time-consuming

00:09:28.233 --> 00:09:31.032
and most difficult aspect of this project.

00:09:31.033 --> 00:09:33.366
The second was that we had to find

00:09:33.367 --> 00:09:35.232
a way to determine the phases,

00:09:35.233 --> 00:09:37.632
and that was not a trivial matter,

00:09:37.633 --> 00:09:40.332
and this is not something I will go into in great detail.

00:09:40.333 --> 00:09:42.866
And third, we needed access to stronger X-ray beams,

00:09:42.867 --> 00:09:46.499
and it was very fortuitous that at that time,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.466
third generation synchrotrons were developed

00:09:49.467 --> 00:09:51.066
that allowed us...

00:09:51.067 --> 00:09:54.999
that produced very hard X-rays

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.032
that were required for this project.

00:09:58.033 --> 00:10:01.732
So, how do we get to greater sample homogeneity?

00:10:01.733 --> 00:10:04.866
Obviously, cutting nucleosomes out

00:10:04.867 --> 00:10:06.566
from native tissues did not work,

00:10:06.567 --> 00:10:09.466
so we had to individually express

00:10:09.467 --> 00:10:11.199
every single histone in bacteria.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:12.932
We then had to refold those histones

00:10:12.933 --> 00:10:15.366
to form what we call a histone octamer,

00:10:15.367 --> 00:10:17.932
which is the protein component of the nucleosome.

00:10:17.933 --> 00:10:20.066
We then had to find a DNA sequence

00:10:20.067 --> 00:10:24.432
that was uniquely suited to position nucleosomes

00:10:24.433 --> 00:10:25.866
and to form nucleosomes.

00:10:25.867 --> 00:10:29.599
And we had to prepare vast amounts of that DNA,

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.366
and that was, in and of itself, a major undertaking,

00:10:32.367 --> 00:10:35.466
and involved a lot of phenol extraction...

00:10:35.467 --> 00:10:38.499
not a lot of fun, I should say.

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:41.866
You then combine those two components,

00:10:41.867 --> 00:10:44.599
and I had to develop methods

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.766
to form the nucleosomes in vitro.

00:10:47.767 --> 00:10:50.099
Once I've done that,

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:52.099
I had to convince myself and others

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:54.399
that these nucleosomes that I made from scratch,

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.266
from bottom up, from their basic components,

00:10:56.267 --> 00:11:00.599
are exactly the same --

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.366
just better than the native nucleosomes

00:11:02.367 --> 00:11:04.199
that we would isolate from tissues.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:07.466
And that required quite a long...

00:11:07.467 --> 00:11:09.632
a long effort in...

00:11:09.633 --> 00:11:12.066
efforts in biochemistry and other approaches.

00:11:12.067 --> 00:11:15.066
But finally, we had nucleosomes that had...

00:11:15.067 --> 00:11:17.332
were unique with DNA sequence and length.

00:11:17.333 --> 00:11:19.732
They had highly uniform histones.

00:11:19.733 --> 00:11:23.732
And furthermore, this approach allowed us to introduce mutants into the histones,

00:11:23.733 --> 00:11:26.299
and this proved to be essential for our efforts

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.032
to determine the phase problem.

00:11:29.033 --> 00:11:31.266
So, all is well. Everybody's happy.

00:11:31.267 --> 00:11:33.499
We should be well on our way to determining

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:35.166
a high resolution structure,

00:11:35.167 --> 00:11:37.032
but of course this was not so.

00:11:37.033 --> 00:11:39.499
As science goes, two steps forward, three steps back.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:41.699
The crystals that we obtained

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.799
from this allegedly much better material

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.432
were actually much worse.

00:11:45.433 --> 00:11:47.232
They diffracted badly.

00:11:47.233 --> 00:11:48.732
Sometimes they didn't crystallize at all.

00:11:48.733 --> 00:11:51.132
We got thin window panes

00:11:51.133 --> 00:11:52.699
that were really not easy to mount at all.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:56.099
And so, it seemed like all our efforts were for naught.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.899
And so, this was really a low point in this whole effort.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:02.199
And I really...

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:03.732
we really had to dig ourselves out from this hole.

00:12:03.733 --> 00:12:06.932
I should point out that the material that we put into these crystals,

00:12:06.933 --> 00:12:10.599
when we analyzed it on native gel electrophoresis,

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:12.432
looks kind of fuzzy.

00:12:12.433 --> 00:12:14.499
And that's just how they looked like.

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:17.732
But in order to find out

00:12:17.733 --> 00:12:19.699
why these crystals all of a sudden

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:23.332
were so ill-behaved and didn't produce...

00:12:23.333 --> 00:12:25.399
didn't... didn't diffract as well,

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:28.632
I had to figure out what was incorporated into the crystal.

00:12:28.633 --> 00:12:31.199
Maybe something had to happen in that long time

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.566
when they refused to crystallize for them...

00:12:33.567 --> 00:12:37.666
so that they could be converted into a form

00:12:37.667 --> 00:12:39.366
that allowed them to crystallize.

00:12:39.367 --> 00:12:41.632
So, one way to do this is to just take your hard-earned crystals,

00:12:41.633 --> 00:12:42.866
and you dissolve them,

00:12:42.867 --> 00:12:44.466
and you analyze them on a gel.

00:12:44.467 --> 00:12:45.866
And much to my surprise,

00:12:45.867 --> 00:12:47.666
I found that rather than being fuzzy,

00:12:47.667 --> 00:12:49.966
like the input material,

00:12:49.967 --> 00:12:51.632
these crystals were actually...

00:12:51.633 --> 00:12:54.799
the contents of the crystals was actually a very nice crisp

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:56.299
and tight band.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:58.399
So, this was really weird.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:12:59.766
What happens?

00:12:59.767 --> 00:13:01.832
There's two possibilities...

00:13:01.833 --> 00:13:03.432
formal possibilities.

00:13:03.433 --> 00:13:05.632
In one possibility,

00:13:05.633 --> 00:13:06.932
we have a mixture of smileys and frownies,

00:13:06.933 --> 00:13:09.432
and only the smileys make it into the crystal.

00:13:09.433 --> 00:13:11.566
So, when I dissolve my crystal,

00:13:11.567 --> 00:13:14.432
I can only see the nice tight band composed of smileys.

00:13:14.433 --> 00:13:17.466
And the frownies would still be

00:13:17.467 --> 00:13:18.832
in what we call a mother liquor.

00:13:18.833 --> 00:13:20.599
This is the liquor surrounding our crystal.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:22.232
In the second possibility,

00:13:22.233 --> 00:13:25.032
it could be that these two forms

00:13:25.033 --> 00:13:26.666
are interchangeable or in equilibrium.

00:13:26.667 --> 00:13:28.532
And it could be that only our smileys

00:13:28.533 --> 00:13:30.066
are incorporated into the crystals

00:13:30.067 --> 00:13:31.999
and thereby shifting the equilibrium,

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.199
pulling the frownies over to the smileys.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:36.666
In this case,

00:13:36.667 --> 00:13:38.932
I would have smileys in my crystals

00:13:38.933 --> 00:13:40.466
and nothing in the mother liquor.

00:13:40.467 --> 00:13:42.399
So, this is a very easy hypothesis to test.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.966
And to our delight,

00:13:44.967 --> 00:13:47.332
we saw that there was really nothing left in the mother liquor.

00:13:47.333 --> 00:13:50.099
And so, this really means that this hypothesis

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:51.966
was correct,

00:13:51.967 --> 00:13:54.332
and now we had to find a way to convince

00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:56.732
all of our frownies to be converted into smileys,

00:13:56.733 --> 00:13:59.666
and that would really solve all of our problems

00:13:59.667 --> 00:14:02.099
and allow us to move ahead in our efforts

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.899
to determine the structure.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.232
So, what are the possible reasons

00:14:06.233 --> 00:14:10.799
for this sample heterogeneity?

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:13.366
We know that our histones are uniform.

00:14:13.367 --> 00:14:15.032
We know our DNA is uniform.

00:14:15.033 --> 00:14:17.599
So, the only variable that was left was the position of the DNA

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.899
on the histone octamer.

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:22.532
And this is illustrated by this little yarn spool, here,

00:14:22.533 --> 00:14:24.499
with the white DNA wrapped around it.

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:28.499
In the top example, the DNA is positioned centrally

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.332
and nice and tightly tucked in.

00:14:30.333 --> 00:14:32.632
In the bottom example,

00:14:32.633 --> 00:14:35.732
I tucked on the left-hand side of my yar... of my...

00:14:35.733 --> 00:14:38.499
of my DNA and pulled it off a little bit.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.899
This leaves a little bit more of the yarn spool exposed,

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:45.132
and leaves some of the DNA sticking out.

00:14:45.133 --> 00:14:47.166
And indeed, this is what we...

00:14:47.167 --> 00:14:48.999
what we believe has happened.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.766
This is shown in this cartoon

00:14:51.767 --> 00:14:53.132
underneath the gel here.

00:14:53.133 --> 00:14:59.099
The three bands, we believe, are...

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:01.299
constitute the central...

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:03.966
centrally positioned nucleosome

00:15:03.967 --> 00:15:05.532
that would be the most fast-moving,

00:15:05.533 --> 00:15:07.199
because it's the most compact.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.066
And then we'd have two nucleosome species

00:15:09.067 --> 00:15:11.366
that have DNA hanging out either on the left side

00:15:11.367 --> 00:15:14.299
or on the right side at different angles,

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.432
and that would change their electrophoretic behavior,

00:15:16.433 --> 00:15:19.632
and that thus would result in those three bands.

00:15:19.633 --> 00:15:23.399
So, how could we convince these two top species

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:27.132
to move to the centrally positioned species?

00:15:27.133 --> 00:15:29.732
Now, we surmise that

00:15:29.733 --> 00:15:31.466
the centrally positioned species

00:15:31.467 --> 00:15:33.699
would be the thermodynamically most stable,

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:35.566
because most of the DNA is bound tightly,

00:15:35.567 --> 00:15:37.666
and so this would be a more stable situation

00:15:37.667 --> 00:15:41.132
than having DNA kind of flopping off the side.

00:15:41.133 --> 00:15:44.732
And so, we figured that

00:15:44.733 --> 00:15:46.999
a simple heating step could actually do the job

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.766
of converting these species into each other.

00:15:48.767 --> 00:15:50.799
And much to our surprise,

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:52.932
just introducing a little bit of thermal energy

00:15:52.933 --> 00:15:57.799
-- heating our samples at 37 degrees for half an hour --

00:15:57.800 --> 00:15:59.699
actually did the job,

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:02.099
and we now got beautifully homogeneous particles

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:06.099
that actually gave rise to beautiful crystals.

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:08.199
And what was even better,

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:10.366
these crystals diffracted to very high resolution,

00:16:10.367 --> 00:16:12.932
giving rise to these diffraction patterns

00:16:12.933 --> 00:16:15.532
that still...

00:16:15.533 --> 00:16:16.966
I get goosebumps when I see them,

00:16:16.967 --> 00:16:20.566
just because they're so nice to look at.

00:16:20.567 --> 00:16:22.766
Now, you think you're done, right?

00:16:22.767 --> 00:16:24.166
But you're really not done.

00:16:24.167 --> 00:16:25.799
Structural biology is an endurance sport,

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.232
maybe like a triathlon.

00:16:28.233 --> 00:16:30.166
So, we've solved the first...

00:16:30.167 --> 00:16:32.066
we solved the first problem,

00:16:32.067 --> 00:16:33.599
the structural homogeneity.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.566
We've obtained well-diffracting crystals.

00:16:35.567 --> 00:16:37.666
Now, we have to solve the phase problem.

00:16:37.667 --> 00:16:41.066
Without going into detail, the phase problem...

00:16:41.067 --> 00:16:44.432
to solve this problem involved

00:16:44.433 --> 00:16:46.532
me making about 25 or 30 mutants in histones,

00:16:46.533 --> 00:16:50.899
isolating each of these mutants independently,

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:53.299
making 25 different flavors of nucleosomes,

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:57.599
and collecting data on these nucleosomes

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:00.199
so that we could obtain

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:02.099
heavy atom derivatives.

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:04.699
And the third hurdle that we had to overcome

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:07.632
was using a high-energy X-ray source.

00:17:07.633 --> 00:17:09.632
These are very weakly diffracting crystals,

00:17:09.633 --> 00:17:12.099
and they require what we call very hard X-rays

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:14.332
so that we could get enough intensity

00:17:14.333 --> 00:17:16.366
from our diffracted crystals.

00:17:16.367 --> 00:17:18.432
And luckily, the third generation synchrotrons

00:17:18.433 --> 00:17:20.666
were under construction

00:17:20.667 --> 00:17:22.399
and were becoming operational just at that time.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:26.266
And synchrotrons are large particle accelerators.

00:17:26.267 --> 00:17:28.399
They're about two kilometers in diameter.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:31.966
This is the one in Grenoble, the ESRF.

00:17:31.967 --> 00:17:34.132
We were among the first users.

00:17:34.133 --> 00:17:36.799
And rumors have it that the first users

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:39.299
used their waiting time

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.632
while the samples were exposed

00:17:41.633 --> 00:17:43.799
to play squash in empty hutches

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.966
and use the smooth concrete floors for rollerblading.

00:17:46.967 --> 00:17:49.632
I would not know anything about these rumors,

00:17:49.633 --> 00:17:53.132
so I will deny all...

00:17:53.133 --> 00:17:54.532
all accounts of this.

00:17:54.533 --> 00:17:56.132
That's all I'll say about it.

00:17:56.133 --> 00:17:58.266
So, now remember what the...

00:17:58.267 --> 00:18:00.766
the result that we get from diffraction experiments

00:18:00.767 --> 00:18:02.966
is not the structure itself.

00:18:02.967 --> 00:18:05.466
It is actually the electron density.

00:18:05.467 --> 00:18:09.466
The electron density without a model built into it

00:18:09.467 --> 00:18:10.866
tells us nothing.

00:18:10.867 --> 00:18:12.566
The electron dens...

00:18:12.567 --> 00:18:14.099
so, then I had to sit for about a year in a dark room,

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.532
painstakingly and manually

00:18:16.533 --> 00:18:19.866
building a three-dimensional structure into this model.

00:18:19.867 --> 00:18:21.466
Now, this was really, really difficult,

00:18:21.467 --> 00:18:24.732
because we had no idea where to start.

00:18:24.733 --> 00:18:28.666
We had no idea

00:18:28.667 --> 00:18:30.466
what the secondary structure of these proteins was.

00:18:30.467 --> 00:18:32.932
We had no idea where the DNA was.

00:18:32.933 --> 00:18:34.499
This is the raw data that we had.

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:36.732
And so, this is like a big three-dimensional puzzle,

00:18:36.733 --> 00:18:39.432
and only when all the parts

00:18:39.433 --> 00:18:41.332
are fit in correctly,

00:18:41.333 --> 00:18:43.332
you can account for all the electron density.

00:18:43.333 --> 00:18:46.299
And so, this is what the final product, then, would look like.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:48.699
And this required, as I said,

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:52.299
about one year of work

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:53.999
in a dark room in front of a computer monitor --

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.366
not a very social experience.

00:18:56.367 --> 00:18:59.832
Here's a detailed...

00:18:59.833 --> 00:19:01.166
a detailed view of the same density.

00:19:01.167 --> 00:19:04.566
And you can see that now we can actually obtain

00:19:04.567 --> 00:19:07.766
the orientation of the main chain and of the sidechains of the protein.

00:19:07.767 --> 00:19:09.699
And this allowed us to really...

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:14.366
to describe how the DNA

00:19:14.367 --> 00:19:16.032
is held so tightly by the histones,

00:19:16.033 --> 00:19:18.432
how the histones interact with each other.

00:19:18.433 --> 00:19:20.599
And this gave the...

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:22.632
gave rise to this iconic picture, here,

00:19:22.633 --> 00:19:25.699
of the final nucleosome structure in two different orientations

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:31.632
that now graces mouse pads and textbooks and T-shirts.

00:19:31.633 --> 00:19:36.299
And it's just generally very, very pretty to look at.

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:38.766
Like all scientific endeavors,

00:19:38.767 --> 00:19:45.499
this particular project took very many years,

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.899
and it was a huge group effort

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:50.066
and couldn't have been done without my colleagues

00:19:50.067 --> 00:19:52.366
at the ETH Zurich,

00:19:52.367 --> 00:20:11.133
with the guidance of Tim Richmond.

